; Parenthesized types.
; - NESTED `(T)` unwraps to `T` (the common case): `nested`'s `(uint)` field is a plain u64.
; - A TOP-LEVEL `foo = (single-type)` is a parenthesized TYPE -> a plain alias (`basic`
;   -> `pub type ... = u64`). `(A / B)` is a type-choice -> enum.
; - A trailing `@`-directive on such a parenthesized wrapper is carried into the inner type, so
;   `newtype_via_parens = (uint) ; @newtype` becomes a real newtype wrapper (NOT a dropped alias).
;   The same holds for a trailing directive on a tagged wrapper (`#6.x(...) ; @newtype`): the outer
;   rule's comment DSL is threaded through the single-type unwrap rather than silently lost.
;   (`holder` references the rules for coverage.)
basic = (uint)
choice = (uint / text)
newtype_via_parens = (uint) ; @newtype
nested = [a: (uint), b: tstr]
holder = [ref_basic: basic, ref_choice: choice, ref_newtype: newtype_via_parens]
